  Phase 2 - Establish Headers, Availability, and the Object Model:
    ☒ Add `include/SimdLib/PartialRegisterFwd.h` with the constrained forward declaration and any target-selected alias approved by the public contract.
    ☒ Add `include/SimdLib/PartialRegister.h` as an independent, Register-shaped initial skeleton; retain only the object-model and invariant-boundary surface needed before the operation-specific implementation work, and document the deferred Register counterparts in the approved operation ledger.
    ☒ Keep `PartialRegister` final, non-polymorphic, non-allocating, and backed by exactly one `Api::vector_t` value with no additional data members or base classes.
    ☒ Initialize the native value to zero and ensure every supported construction path establishes the inactive-lane invariant.
    ☒ Add compile-time metadata for physical width, physical byte count, native lane count, active lane count, active byte count, and inactive lane count.
    ☒ Add a reusable internal active-lane mask or equivalent compile-time value derived only from the template arguments; do not store it per object.
    ☒ Use Register-style aggregate native storage with no custom constructors; distinguish already-clean operation results from native imports that require normalization at method boundaries.
    ☒ Add a checks-enabled invariant validator that verifies inactive lanes by bit representation and returns its input unchanged for optimized builds.
    ☒ Ensure every new class and method has a Doxygen contract covering logical lanes, inactive-lane behavior, availability, result type, and preconditions.
    ☒ Add first-include, umbrella-header, unavailable-language-mode, required-interface, and availability probes corresponding to the existing Register header boundaries.
    ☒ Decide whether the existing `SimdLib::Register` CMake interface target owns both register-shaped types or whether a separate `SimdLib::PartialRegister` target is needed; do not raise the core C++20 target requirement.
    ☒ Add object-model assertions for size, alignment, standard layout, trivial copy/move/destruction, trivial copyability, and absence of runtime metadata for every supported type/width cell.

  Phase 3 - Implement PartialRegisterMask and Predicate Semantics:
    ☒ Decide the companion predicate spelling and template shape, with `PartialRegisterMask<element_t, register_width, active_lane_count>` as the default design.
    ☒ Implement the predicate as an independent final one-native-register aggregate whose documented direct-initialization precondition and public operation results preserve all-bits-zero inactive mask lanes.
    ☒ Define the RegisterMask-compatible `any()`, `all()`, `none()`, and `bits()` surface over active lanes only.
    ☒ Ensure `all()` can return true when every active lane is true even though every inactive lane remains false.
    ☒ Implement the RegisterMask-compatible `operator&`, `operator|`, `operator^`, and `operator~` surface with inactive-result normalization where the intrinsic result is not closed over zero, especially bitwise NOT.
    ☒ Implement the RegisterMask-compatible `select(when_true, when_false)` surface so the returned PartialRegister preserves inactive zeros regardless of predicate or source values.
    ☒ Preserve the deliberate absence of PartialRegisterMask `operator&=`, `operator|=`, and `operator^=`.
    ☒ Sanitize true inactive predicate lanes in `from_native()` and validate the documented direct aggregate-initialization precondition at checked observation boundaries.
    ☒ Add PartialRegisterMask concepts or extend the structural concept inventory without weakening the complete-register guarantees of `IRegisterMask`.
    ☒ Complete the partial-predicate qualification coverage below; do not treat a compile-only fixture or structural assertions as generated-code or operational constexpr proof.
    ☒ Add operational constexpr assertions for `bits()`, `any()`, `all()`, `none()`, Boolean composition, and `select()` at representative partial extents.
    ☒ Add mixed-predicate runtime selection tests that choose both `when_true` and `when_false` lanes, verify each active lane, and verify every inactive result lane is bitwise zero.
    ☒ Add checks-enabled negative coverage proving noncanonical active native predicate lanes are rejected, while true inactive native predicate lanes are sanitized at the import boundary.
    ☒ Add compile-time surface assertions that `PartialRegisterMask` deliberately has no `operator&=`, `operator|=`, or `operator^=`.
    ☒ Expand runtime predicate coverage across the supported element-type matrix and representative interior active counts, in addition to the existing one-lane and one-fewer-than-native boundaries.
    ☒ Replace the compile-only predicate fixture with paired wrapper/raw generated-code fixtures, mandatory disassembly comparisons, and SSE4.2-128/AVX2-256 coverage; the raw mirror must include the same required inactive-suffix normalization.

  Phase 4 - Implement Construction, Transfer, and Observation:
    ☐ Implement `zero()` with every native lane zero.
    ☐ Implement `broadcast(value)` over active lanes only and explicitly zero the inactive suffix.
    ☐ Implement logical lane-list and fixed-array construction with exactly `active_lane_count` source elements in low-to-high order.
    ☐ Implement aligned and unaligned element loads from exactly the approved logical extent without reading beyond caller-provided active data.
    ☐ Implement byte loads according to the approved active-byte transfer contract without reading beyond that extent.
    ☐ Implement aligned and unaligned stores that write only active logical elements and leave surrounding destination canaries unchanged.
    ☐ Implement byte stores according to the approved active-byte transfer contract and verify surrounding byte canaries.
    ☐ Implement `to_array()` with active logical extent rather than native lane extent.
    ☐ Constrain `lane<index>()` and `with_lane<index>()` to active lanes only.
    ☐ Ensure `with_lane<index>()` cannot alter the inactive suffix and preserves it bitwise.
    ☐ Add positive tests for every active count, including one active lane and one fewer than the native lane count, across all supported element types and widths.
    ☐ Add compile-failure probes for zero or excessive active counts, oversized lane lists, full-register transfer extents, inactive lane indices, implicit scalar/native conversion, and unavailable width cells; add checks-enabled coverage for violated direct native aggregate preconditions.

  Phase 5 - Implement Arithmetic and Specialized Operations:
    ☐ Implement addition, subtraction, multiplication, negation, minimum, maximum, absolute value, square root, average, saturating arithmetic, and multiply-add forms according to the operation ledger.
    ☐ Retain an unnormalized result only when the selected `Api` operation is proven to map zero inactive inputs to all-bits-zero inactive outputs for every supported element type and compiler profile.
    ☐ Normalize results for operations whose inactive output is not closed over all-bits-zero inputs.
    ☐ For division and modulus, provide valid neutral inactive divisor lanes before invoking `Api`, preserve existing active-lane preconditions, and restore the inactive result to zero.
    ☐ Define whether magnitude, checked magnitude, and normalization operate over active lanes only; ensure inactive lanes do not contribute to scalar intermediates or error conditions.
    ☐ Define the logical output layout and active-lane count for adjacent, horizontal, SAD, multi-SAD, dot-product, and other lane-combining operations before implementing them.
    ☐ Return `Register`, `PartialRegister`, or a scalar deliberately for each type-changing or lane-reducing specialized operation; do not choose a result solely because it matches the copied signature.
    ☐ Ensure scalar reductions, minimum/maximum positions, and reduction predicates inspect only active lanes and report logical indices.
    ☐ Add independent scalar-oracle tests for active results and bitwise-zero assertions for every inactive result lane.
    ☐ Add checks-enabled negative tests for active-lane division, modulus, normalization, and other retained preconditions without triggering failures from sanitized inactive operands.

  Phase 6 - Implement Bitwise, Shift, and Comparison Operations:
    ☐ Implement bitwise AND, OR, XOR, NOT, and AND-NOT with explicit inactive-lane closure or normalization decisions.
    ☐ Mask bitwise NOT results so inactive lanes remain all-bits-zero.
    ☐ Define compact movemask and lane-sign-bit results over active lanes only, with no observable bits for inactive lanes.
    ☐ Implement per-lane logical, arithmetic, and immediate shifts while preserving existing active-lane count and precondition behavior.
    ☐ Define whole-register byte and bit shifts over the logical active payload rather than accidentally treating inactive padding as active data.
    ☐ Normalize shift results whenever active values can move into the inactive suffix.
    ☐ Implement comparisons through PartialRegisterMask and ensure every inactive predicate lane remains false, including equality of two inactive zero lanes.
    ☐ Implement scalar equality and inequality over active lanes only and preserve the existing floating NaN and signed-zero contract for those lanes.
    ☐ Add boundary tests for active counts that do not fill a byte-shift or half-register grouping cleanly.
    ☐ Add runtime, constexpr, compile-time surface, and compile-failure coverage for every retained bitwise, shift, comparison, and immediate-count form.

  Phase 7 - Implement Rearrangement and Conversion Operations:
    ☐ Define logical shuffle selectors over `active_lane_count`, including whether output selectors may explicitly request a zero-filled lane.
    ☐ Define byte-shuffle selectors over the active byte extent and ensure selector results cannot populate inactive bytes.
    ☐ Define `unpack_low`, `unpack_high`, blend, and half-local shuffle behavior when the active count is odd or smaller than the intrinsic grouping.
    ☐ Resolve and document every copied rearrangement's active-lane meaning; do not omit a Register method merely because its partial semantics require additional work.
    ☐ Define `lower_half()` result types for active counts below, equal to, and above the destination native capacity.
    ☐ Define bit-cast result active counts from the number of active source bits; reject target element types for which that logical bit extent cannot be represented as complete target lanes unless an explicit padding rule is approved.
    ☐ Define numeric conversion result active counts independently from bit-cast rules and ensure converted inactive inputs do not create observable output lanes.
    ☐ Define widening and narrowing operations by the number of consumed active source lanes and produced active target lanes.
    ☐ Preserve zero inactive lanes after every shuffle, unpack, blend, extraction, bit cast, numeric conversion, and widening operation.
    ☐ Add exhaustive selector tests for small logical lane counts, representative cross-half tests, conversion source/target matrix tests, and compile-failure probes for invalid selectors and result cells.

  Phase 8 - Complete the Public Surface and Integration:
    ☐ Add or adapt structural concepts so the complete PartialRegister surface is audited without claiming complete-register lane semantics.
    ☐ Add aliases only after the primary template and native-width naming are approved; keep aliases grouped by their required instruction-set preconditions.
    ☐ Add PartialRegister and its predicate to the focused and umbrella public headers under the existing C++23 availability boundary.
    ☐ Add standalone first-include probes for every new public header.
    ☐ Add ODR and multi-translation-unit coverage.
    ☐ Add an external-consumer example that loads a partial logical value, performs representative operations, verifies inactive-lane behavior through public observations, and stores only active data.
    ☐ Register all positive, negative, constexpr, header, availability, ODR, consumer, and runtime targets in CMake for SSE4.2 and AVX2 where supported.
    ☐ Ensure installed-package consumers receive the new headers and target requirements without depending on source-tree-only paths.

  Phase 9 - Qualify ABI, Generated Code, and Performance:
    ☐ Create PartialRegister generated-code fixtures paired with direct `Api` raw mirrors that perform the same required masking, neutral-operand preparation, and normalization.
    ☐ Do not compare invariant-preserving PartialRegister code against a raw baseline that omits required inactive-lane work.
    ☐ Cover construction, transfer, unary and binary arithmetic, division/modulus, comparisons and mask use, reductions, shifts, rearrangements, conversions, and representative composed expressions.
    ☐ Add non-inlined ABI mirrors for value parameters, value returns, reassignment, mask results, scalar results, type-changing results, opaque calls, and register-pressure cases.
    ☐ Prove that each PartialRegister specialization occupies and passes exactly one native register wherever the corresponding Register ABI contract permits it.
    ☐ Require wrapper-versus-raw generated code to match after existing normalization except for documented compiler-specific sequences that are necessary to preserve the partial invariant.
    ☐ Record every accepted exception with compiler, version, ISA profile, exact symbol, instruction difference, reason, and retention test.
    ☐ Add permanent symbol ownership and generated-code audit records equivalent to the Register qualification inventory.
    ☐ Add supplemental benchmarks for representative active counts and operations where invariant maintenance may add instructions; keep benchmark results separate from correctness and generated-code gates.
    ☐ Run strict Release generated-code gates before using benchmark results to make performance claims.

  Phase 10 - Documentation, Full Validation, and Cleanup:
    ☐ Add a PartialRegister design and qualification document that records the approved contract, operation ledger, result-type rules, invariant-preservation strategies, compiler matrix, and accepted generated-code exceptions.
    ☐ Update the public namespace and technical-reference documentation with the distinction among Register, PartialRegister, SimdVector, and collection-level tail handling.
    ☐ Document representative construction, arithmetic, comparison, rearrangement, conversion, and store workflows using only public APIs.
    ☐ Update the Register proposal and operation matrix only where they must route readers to the new sibling type; do not weaken the complete-register contract.
    ☐ Run formatting and static analysis over every added or modified production and test source.
    ☐ Run focused PartialRegister runtime, constexpr, compile-failure, header, ODR, consumer, ABI, and generated-code tests for every supported native compiler and ISA cell.
    ☐ Run the complete native MSVC and clang-cl build/test matrix.
    ☐ Run the complete pinned GCC and Clang container build/test matrix, including sanitizer coverage.
    ☐ Build and run supplemental benchmarks after all strict correctness and generated-code gates pass.
    ☐ Verify the installed-package consumer boundary and inspect the installed header set.
    ☐ Audit every copied Register declaration against the operation ledger and verify no complete-register wording, lane extent, mask assumption, result type, or unsafe construction path remains unintentionally.
    ☐ Search for stale temporary names, unregistered tests, unowned generated-code symbols, and references that incorrectly describe PartialRegister as a Register subclass.
    ☐ Run repository validation tooling and `git diff --check`.
    ☐ Preserve unrelated worktree and index state; do not stash or commit changes as part of checklist execution.
    ☐ Record exact compiler versions, commands, test counts, generated-code results, benchmark artifact paths, sanitizer results, package evidence, and any justified remaining limitations in the qualification document.
    ☐ Complete this checklist only when every supported operation has an approved ledger entry, direct active-lane behavior evidence, direct inactive-lane invariant evidence, and all required validation cells are green.
